---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <p>For fun only. ToyTools does not support splitting payments to dodge a fee, a tax, or a reporting rule. This is not tax, legal, or payments advice.</p>
  <p>We do not know whether NPCI, a bank, or a UPI app treats several payments as one total. Do not use this for that.</p>

  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>This UPI split is a meme calculator, not advice.</p>
    <p>You type whole rupees. The page either says one payment, or it groups the 1999 amounts into one line. It does not pay anyone.</p>
    <p>People open it while checking the viral 2000 rupee claim. The claim is false. Therefore the line is a joke, not a plan.</p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the UPI 1999 Split</a>
  </section>

  <section id="government-tax">
    <h2>Is there a government tax on UPI above 2000 rupees? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>There is no government tax on a UPI payment above 2000 rupees.</p>
    <p>The Finance Ministry called GST-on-UPI-above-2000 reports false. A customer does not owe a 2000 rupee UPI tax because that tax does not exist.</p>
    <p>However, a post can still sound official. Read the press note, then look at the chunks as arithmetic. Do not read them as a way past a rule.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="pib-delhi">
    <h2>What did PIB Delhi say on 15 Sep 2026? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>Person-to-person UPI stays free at any amount.</p>
    <p>MDR is not a tax and is not collected by the government or NPCI. A 0.4% MDR can apply to some person-to-merchant payments above 2000 rupees. Customers are not supposed to pay that MDR.</p>
    <p>
      The source is the
      <a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleseDetailm.aspx?PRID=2310586">PIB Delhi release from 15 Sep 2026</a>.
    </p>
    <p>That is the whole rule set this page will state. It does not add dates, rates, or extra conditions on top of that note.</p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="There is no 2000 rupee UPI tax">
      <p>The Finance Ministry called GST-on-UPI-above-2000 reports false.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="how-the-split-works">
    <h2>How does the integer split work? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>It subtracts 1999 until the remainder is 1999 or less.</p>
    <p>The threshold is locked at 2000. The chunk is locked at 1999. There is no control for either number, because a meme is not a tax planner.</p>
    <p>For example, 2001 becomes 1999 plus 2. For example, 3998 is 1999 plus 1999. For example, 10000 is five chunks of 1999 plus 5, and 1999 times 5 is 9995.</p>
    <p>Negative numbers, zero, and anything that is not a number are rejected. Paise are rejected too. Whole rupees only.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="five-thousand">
    <h2>What does 5000 become? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>5000 becomes 1999, then 1999, then 1002.</p>
    <p>The page reads 2 times 1,999, then 1,002, and 3 payments. The parts sum to 5000. It does not print one row per chunk.</p>
    <p>The last chunk is whatever is left. It can be under 1999, as 1002 is. It can also be exactly 1999 when the total divides cleanly, as 3998 does.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="under-2000">
    <h2>What if the total is under 2000? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>A total under 2000 is one payment.</p>
    <p>The line reads: one payment of that amount, nothing to split. 2000 itself uses that same line. The split starts only above 2000, and even then it is still the same rupees.</p>
    <p>A total above 10,00,000 is refused. The page says so. 10,00,000 itself is still grouped, and the parts still add up.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="hide-the-total">
    <h2>Does splitting hide the total? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>Splitting does not hide the total from the person you paid.</p>
    <p>The chunks are a picture of one number. They do not shrink it, and they do not hide it. We do not know whether NPCI, a bank, or a UPI app treats several payments as one total.</p>
    <p>Do not use this page for that. It will not tell you how to send the chunks, and it will not say that a split works as a trick.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="versus-tax">
    <h2>UPI 1999 Split versus a tax calculator <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>This UPI split versus a tax calculator is a joke, not a rate.</p>
    <p>The tax calculator applies a percent you type. The tip calculator adds a percent you choose. The discount calculator takes a percent off a price. Unlike those tools, this page has no rate field.</p>
    <p>The difference between them is the job. Those three answer a percent question. This one answers a meme with integer chunks. Use the other tools when you actually have a rate.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>Two mistakes treat a joke list as if it were a rule.</p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Reading the chunk list as a fee dodge">
      <p>The list is arithmetic. It is not a plan, and this page will not help you send it.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Believing a post about a 2000 rupee tax as fact">
      <p>A customer does not owe that tax. Splitting does not hide the total.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <p>Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded. The number you type stays in the tab, and the result does not wait on a server.</p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
